  "description": "meme-id: jokes-don-t-kill-people; hasOverallIntent: hateful; overall-intent-description: The meme depicts a group of men, visually identifiable as Muslim men based on their attire (keffiyehs, headscarves), in an angry, aggressive pose. The text juxtaposes two statements: 'jokes don't kill people' and 'muslims who are offended by jokes kill people.' This creates a false equivalence and scapegoats an entire religious group for acts of violence, implying that the mere act of being offended by jokes is a justification for violence. This is a harmful and hateful stereotype that directly attacks Muslims by linking their religious identity to violence and aggression, which is a common trope in Islamophobia. The intent is to delegitimize the right of Muslims to feel offended by certain jokes, while simultaneously implying that their anger is inherently dangerous and responsible for real-world violence. The image and text together function as a prejudiced argument that uses the emotional reaction of a group to justify a violent outcome, thereby promoting hatred and fear toward Muslims.",
